  - Use Frakment commercially only after you get one pull request
    merged into the main repository. That is the only path.

  - Always keep the attribution. Always mark modifications.

====================================================================
1. DEFINITIONS
====================================================================

"Software" means the source code, components, CLI, MCP server, agent
skills, documentation, and any associated files in this repository,
whether used in original or modified form.

"Personal Use" means use by an individual for personal projects,
learning, experimentation, hobby work, or internal tools that do not
generate revenue.

"Educational Use" means use by students, teachers, or institutions
for teaching, coursework, academic research, or non-commercial
educational activities.

"Commercial Use" means any use that:

  (a) is part of a product, service, or website that generates revenue
      directly or indirectly (including ads, subscriptions, paid
      features, consulting, or paid support);
  (b) is deployed in production by a for-profit entity; or
  (c) is used to build something that is sold, licensed, or monetized
      in any form.

"Qualifying Contribution" means a pull request that has been
reviewed and merged into the main branch of
github.com/hanshapd/frakment, where all of the following are true:

  (a) The commit author is you (your GitHub username or email is
      recorded in the commit metadata).
  (b) The pull request makes at least one substantive change: fixes a
      bug, adds a feature, adds or corrects documentation, adds a
      test, translates content, or fixes a typo.
  (c) The pull request has not been reverted within 30 days of being
      merged.

There is no minimum number of lines changed. A single-line typo fix
qualifies. A documentation clarification qualifies. A test addition
qualifies.

A pull request that was closed without merging does NOT qualify. A
pull request that was merged and later reverted within 30 days does
NOT qualify.

